# Question 4: Communication and Continuous Integration Rules

## a. Communication Rules

| Area | Rule / Guideline | Details & Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Primary Platform** | Centralized Team Communication | All project-related communication should be conducted through the agreed team communication platform. |
| **Issue Management** | Defect & Task Tracking | GitHub Issues should be used to report bugs, assign development tasks, and track project-related problems. |
| **Issue Specifications** | Descriptive Reporting | Team members should clearly describe the issue, expected outcome, and relevant module when creating an issue. |
| **Status Updates** | Progress Reporting | Team members should provide regular updates on their assigned tasks and inform the team early about any problems or delays. |
| **Technical Consensus** | Prior Consultation | Technical decisions that affect the system architecture, database, coding standards, or development workflow should be discussed with the team before implementation. |
| **Pull Requests** | Contextual Documentation | Pull Requests should include a clear description of the changes made and the related issue or task where applicable. |
| **Code Review** | Mandatory Peer Review | Team members should review and respond to Pull Request comments before the code is merged. |
| **Knowledge Sharing** | Centralized Documentation | Important project decisions and changes should be documented in GitHub so that they can be referred to by all team members. |
| **Standard Terminology** | Domain Dictionary Alignment | Team members should use the approved domain terminology consistently, such as `Customer`, `Staff`, `Product`, `Order`, `stockQuantity`, and `totalAmount`. |
| **Standard Alterations** | Team-wide Notification | Any changes to the agreed coding standards, system structure, or development workflow should be communicated to the entire team. |

